  "bubbleCo2Description": "Soil respiration releases carbon dioxide when microbes and roots decompose organic matter and produce energy. This is a sign of healthy microbial activity. The rate of respiration depends on temperature, moisture, and C content.",
  "bubbleN2oDescription": "Nitrous oxide is released when denitrifying bacteria reduce nitrate in anaerobic (oxygen-free) conditions. This happens especially in waterlogged soil. N2O is a potent greenhouse gas (298x CO2) - a sign of a problem!",
  "ionNitrateDescription": "Negatively charged, highly mobile nitrogen ion. Nitrate moves with water and can leach into groundwater or be lost to the atmosphere through denitrification.",
  "ionPhosphateDescription": "Negatively charged phosphorus ion. Essential for energy (ATP) but moves very slowly in soil due to strong binding to minerals.",
  "ionPotassiumDescription": "Positive ion regulating plant water balance and stomata. Held by soil's cation exchange capacity (CEC).",
  "ionCalciumDescription": "Crucial for cell wall stability and signaling. Moves slowly with water flow.",
  "ionMagnesiumDescription": "Central part of the chlorophyll molecule. Vital for photosynthesis.",
  "bacteriaDescription": "Soil bacteria are the fastest decomposers and the engines of nutrient cycling. They operate near the roots (rhizosphere), decompose organic matter and release nutrients to plants. Nitrifying bacteria convert ammonium to nitrate.",
  "fungiDescription": "Fungal mycelia form underground networks that transport water and nutrients over long distances. Mycorrhizae form symbioses with plants. Fungi decompose difficult-to-decompose compounds such as lignin and cellulose.",
  "actinomycetesDescription": "Streptomyces group bacteria that produce antibiotics and decompose difficult-to-decompose compounds. They produce geosmin - the cause of the earthy smell. Slower than bacteria, but more efficient at decomposing difficult compounds.",
