  "missionNoActiveScenario": "No active scenario",
  "briefingCompactionProblem": "Soil structure is severely compacted: bulk density has climbed up to 1700 kg/m³ and porosity has dropped below 30%. Water cannot infiltrate deeper layers, forming surface puddles. Plant roots cannot penetrate the hard clay pan due to excessive mechanical resistance.",
  "briefingCompactionStep1": "Open the right control panel and perform topsoil tillage to loosen the compaction layer.",
  "briefingCompactionStep2": "Sow a deep-rooted cover crop whose taproots drill biopores into the subsoil.",
  "briefingCompactionStep3": "Avoid heavy downpours: keep precipitation moderate so enough soil air remains and surface slaking is prevented.",
  "briefingCompactionStep4": "Monitor saturated hydraulic conductivity (kSat) and porosity development in the left HUD dashboard.",
  "briefingCompactionScience": "According to the van Genuchten model and 1D Richards equation, hydraulic conductivity drops by orders of magnitude in compacted soil. Root elongation stops when soil mechanical shear strength exceeds root cell turgor.",
  "briefingLeachingProblem": "Coarse sandy soil (70% sand) contains an excessive pool of free nitrate (100 mg/kg). Because sand's cation exchange capacity (CEC) and water retention capacity are very low, heavy rains flush nitrate directly into the groundwater layer, causing nutrient waste and eutrophication.",
  "briefingLeachingStep1": "Establish rapid biomass crops to assimilate free nitrate before major rainfall.",
  "briefingLeachingStep2": "Keep rainfall low or disable Auto Weather to prevent massive percolation.",
  "briefingLeachingStep3": "Incorporate organic matter to elevate microbial carbon, triggering nitrate immobilization into microbial biomass.",
  "briefingLeachingStep4": "Enable Nitrogen Cycle from the bottom bar to inspect nitrate ion trajectory vectors.",
  "briefingLeachingScience": "Nitrate (NO₃⁻) is an anion not bound electrostatically by clay minerals or humus. Transport follows the advection-dispersion equation along with gravitational water flow.",
  "briefingLockProblem": "A high volume of carbon-rich straw was added to the soil, driving the C/N ratio above the critical threshold of 25. Heterotrophic microbes consume all available mineral nitrogen (ammonium and nitrate) to decompose carbon, starving the crop of essential nitrogen.",
  "briefingLockStep1": "Apply mineral nitrogen fertilizer from the control panel to supply readily available N to crops.",
  "briefingLockStep2": "Raise temperature or optimize moisture to accelerate microbial respiration and decomposition turnover.",
  "briefingLockStep3": "Open Nanovision microscope to monitor stomatal recovery and photosystem revival.",
  "briefingLockStep4": "Track the C/N ratio in the HUD dashboard and watch for nitrogen release through microbial turnover (necromass).",
  "briefingLockScience": "Microbial biomass maintains a stoichiometric C/N ratio (~8:1). With surplus available carbon, microbes immobilize all inorganic nitrogen. Remineralization triggers once organic residue C/N falls below 20.",
  "briefingWaterloggingProblem": "Soil is completely water-saturated and pore air space is exhausted. Because gas diffusion in water is 10,000 times slower than in air, soil dissolved oxygen depletes within hours. Redox potential plunges into negative values (-150 mV), initiating denitrification and methanogenesis.",
  "briefingWaterloggingStep1": "Immediately cease all irrigation and ensure rainfall is set to 0 mm.",
  "briefingWaterloggingStep2": "Increase solar radiation (PAR) and air temperature to boost soil water loss via evapotranspiration.",
  "briefingWaterloggingStep3": "Track gas emissions (N₂O and CH₄) in the HUD dashboard and watch redox potential climb back toward aerobic range (> 300 mV).",
  "briefingWaterloggingStep4": "Inspect root system: avoid root rot and cell death induced by prolonged anoxia.",
  "briefingWaterloggingScience": "The thermodynamic redox ladder (Nernst equation) indicates that after O₂ depletion, electrons flow to nitrate (NO₃⁻ → N₂O/N₂), manganese and iron oxides, and ultimately to methane (CH₄).",
  "briefingDefaultProblem": "Balance soil physics and biochemistry to sustain vigorous crop growth.",
  "briefingDefaultStep1": "Inspect soil profile state from the left HUD dashboard.",
  "briefingDefaultStep2": "Adjust environmental drivers and management in the right control panel.",
  "briefingDefaultStep3": "Track sustainability metrics and fulfill scenario objectives.",
  "briefingDefaultScience": "The SPAC continuum links soil pore physics, plant physiology, and atmospheric vapor pressure deficit.",
  "quickGuideTabBiochemistry": "Biochemistry",
  "quickGuideTabGlossary": "Glossary",
  "quickGuideSubtitle": "SPAC simulator features and scientific foundation",
  "quickGuideClose": "Close Guide",
  "quickGuideCardUiDashboardTitle": "HUD Dashboard (Left Sidebar)",
  "quickGuideCardUiDashboardBody": "Displays layer-by-layer soil metrics: volumetric water content (θ), temperature, pH, electrical conductivity (EC), and redox potential (Eh). Also includes the Soil Texture Triangle.",
  "quickGuideCardUiControlsTitle": "Control Panel & Interventions (Right Sidebar)",
  "quickGuideCardUiControlsBody": "Adjust atmospheric conditions (rain, PAR solar radiation, temperature, CO₂) and management practices like irrigation, N and P fertilization, cover crops, and tillage.",
  "quickGuideCardUiTimelineTitle": "Timeline & Speed Control (Bottom)",
  "quickGuideCardUiTimelineBody": "Pause simulation to inspect soil profile, adjust playback speed (1x, 2x, 5x, 10x), or scrub timeline backwards to review crop response.",
  "quickGuideCardUiNanovisionTitle": "Nanovision Microscope (Bottom / Hotspots)",
  "quickGuideCardUiNanovisionBody": "Explore cellular biophysics: stomata (400x), stem xylem (200x), apical meristems (800x), root cells (400x), rhizosphere (600x), and microbes (1200x).",
  "quickGuideCardSciSpacTitle": "SPAC — Soil-Plant-Atmosphere Continuum",
  "quickGuideCardSciSpacBody": "Water moves down a gradient of decreasing chemical water potential (ψ) from wet soil (ψ ≈ -0.03 MPa) to roots, up xylem conduits, and evaporates through stomata into atmosphere (ψ ≈ -100 MPa).",
  "quickGuideCardSciHydraulicsTitle": "Richards Equation & van Genuchten",
  "quickGuideCardSciHydraulicsBody": "Governs unsaturated fluid flow through porous soil matrix combining gravitational potential and matric suction pressure.",
  "quickGuideCardSciBeerLambertTitle": "Canopy Radiation Absorption (Beer-Lambert)",
  "quickGuideCardSciBeerLambertBody": "Solar PAR attenuates exponentially through plant canopy according to Leaf Area Index (LAI) and extinction coefficient k.",
  "quickGuideCardBioSomTitle": "Carbon Dynamics: MAOM vs POM",
  "quickGuideCardBioSomBody": "MAOM (Mineral-Associated Organic Matter) is stable humus protected on clay surfaces lasting centuries. POM (Particulate Organic Matter) is coarse, rapidly cycling plant residue.",
  "quickGuideCardBioCnTitle": "C/N Ratio & Nitrogen Lock",
  "quickGuideCardBioCnBody": "When C/N > 25 (e.g. straw), microbes immobilize mineral nitrogen. When C/N < 20 (e.g. legumes or manure), net mineralization releases nitrogen to plants.",
  "quickGuideCardBioRedoxTitle": "Redox Potential (Eh) & Electron Acceptors",
  "quickGuideCardBioRedoxBody": "Aerobic respiration operates above +300 mV using O₂. When waterlogged, microbes reduce NO₃⁻, Fe/Mn oxides, SO₄²⁻, and finally produce methane at -150 mV.",
  "quickGuideCardTipWateringTitle": "Optimize Irrigation Timing",
  "quickGuideCardTipWateringBody": "Avoid over-irrigation in heavy clay to prevent structural slaking and root rot. Maintain moisture between field capacity and wilting point.",
  "quickGuideCardTipFertilizationTitle": "Split Fertilizer Applications",
  "quickGuideCardTipFertilizationBody": "Apply nitrogen in synchronization with crop uptake demands to minimize nitrate leaching during rainy periods.",
  "quickGuideCardTipCoverCropsTitle": "Utilize Cover Crops",
  "quickGuideCardTipCoverCropsBody": "Cover crops scavenge residual nitrates, generate subsoil biopores, and fuel the soil microbial carbon bank.",
  "quickGuideCardGlosLaiTitle": "LAI — Leaf Area Index",
  "quickGuideCardGlosLaiBody": "Ratio of one-sided green leaf area per unit ground surface area (m²/m²).",
  "quickGuideCardGlosVpdTitle": "VPD — Vapor Pressure Deficit (kPa)",
  "quickGuideCardGlosVpdBody": "Difference between saturated and actual vapor pressure in the air. Drives atmospheric evaporative suction.",
  "quickGuideCardGlosCecTitle": "CEC — Cation Exchange Capacity",
  "quickGuideCardGlosCecBody": "Total capacity of a soil to hold exchangeable cations (Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, K⁺, NH₄⁺) on negatively charged clay and humus.",
  "quickGuideCardGlosParTitle": "PAR — Photosynthetically Active Radiation",
  "quickGuideCardGlosParBody": "Solar spectral waveband from 400 to 700 nm utilized by plant chlorophyll for photosynthesis.",
  "quickGuideCardGlosStomataTitle": "Stomatal Conductance (gs)",
  "quickGuideCardGlosStomataBody": "Rate of CO₂ entry and water vapor exit through stomata, tightly governed by leaf turgor and abscisic acid.",
  "quickGuideCardGlosEcTitle": "EC — Electrical Conductivity (dS/m)",
  "quickGuideCardGlosEcBody": "Measure of soluble dissolved salts and ions in soil solution.",
  "briefingButton": "BRIEFING",
  "quickGuideUiDashboardTitle": "HUD Dashboard (Left Sidebar)",
  "quickGuideUiDashboardBody": "Access layer-specific soil metrics: water content (θ), temperature, pH, electrical conductivity (EC), and redox potential (Eh). Also includes the Soil Texture Triangle.",
  "quickGuideUiControlsTitle": "Control Panel & Interventions (Right Sidebar)",
  "quickGuideUiControlsBody": "Adjust weather conditions (rain, PAR solar radiation, temperature, CO₂) and agricultural management such as irrigation, N and P fertilization, cover crops, and soil tillage.",
  "quickGuideUiTimelineTitle": "Timeline & Speed Control (Bottom)",
  "quickGuideUiTimelineBody": "Pause simulation to inspect soil profile, adjust playback speed (1x, 2x, 5x, 10x), or scrub timeline backwards to review development caused by interventions.",
  "quickGuideUiNanovisionTitle": "Nanovision Microscope (Bottom / Hotspots)",
  "quickGuideUiNanovisionBody": "Delve into cellular biophysics: observe leaf stomata (400x), stem xylem (200x), apical meristems (800x), root cellular structures (400x), rhizosphere (600x), and microbes (1200x).",
  "quickGuideSciRichardsTitle": "1D Richards Equation & Capillarity",
  "quickGuideSciRichardsBody": "Soil water flow is driven by pressure head differences and matric water potential (van Genuchten model). Water percolates downward by gravity and matric suction, while capillary forces pull water toward the evaporation surface in dry soil.",
  "quickGuideSciPhotosynthesisTitle": "FvCB Photosynthesis & Tardieu Stomatal Model",
  "quickGuideSciPhotosynthesisBody": "Crop photosynthesis is driven by solar radiation (PAR), intercellular CO₂, and water availability. As soil dries, the plant closes stomata to prevent dehydration, which curtails carbon assimilation.",
  "quickGuideSciSpacTitle": "SPAC Continuum (Soil-Plant-Atmosphere Continuum)",
  "quickGuideSciSpacBody": "Water forms an unbroken liquid column from soil pores through root endodermis and xylem vessels to leaf stomata and into the atmosphere, propelled along a descending water potential gradient.",
  "quickGuideBioNitrogenTitle": "Nitrogen Cycle: Mineralization & Nitrification",
  "quickGuideBioNitrogenBody": "Organic nitrogen is mineralized by microbes into ammonium (NH₄⁺), which is oxidized into nitrate (NO₃⁻). Nitrate is readily absorbed by roots but leaches easily into groundwater during heavy precipitation.",
  "quickGuideBioRedoxTitle": "Anaerobiosis, Redox State & Denitrification",
  "quickGuideBioRedoxBody": "When soil is saturated, dissolved oxygen plunges. Microbes switch electron acceptors to nitrate and alternate oxides (TEA Redox ladder), generating nitrous oxide (N₂O) and methane (CH₄).",
  "quickGuideBioCarbonTitle": "Carbon Pump & Mycorrhizae (POM / MAOM)",
  "quickGuideBioCarbonBody": "Root exudates stimulate microbial activity (Priming Effect). Mycorrhizal fungi exchange plant carbon for soil phosphorus and nitrogen. Microbial necromass binds to mineral surfaces as stable carbon (MAOM).",
  "quickGuideTipCompactionTitle": "Compacted Clay Management",
  "quickGuideTipCompactionBody": "Apply shallow cultivation to loosen the surface crusted pan and sow deep-rooted cover crops. Avoid heavy machinery on wet soil and nourish soil biota with organic matter.",
  "quickGuideTipLeachingTitle": "Nitrate Leaching Prevention",
  "quickGuideTipLeachingBody": "Avoid high single-dose fertilization before forecasted rain. Integrate mineral N with organic residues and deploy cover or catch crops to capture excess nitrate in the root zone.",
  "quickGuideTipLogicLabTitle": "Logic Lab & Scientific Equations",
  "quickGuideTipLogicLabBody": "Test biophysical equations and parameter sensitivities by opening the Logic Lab menu from the top bar. Inspect Michaelis-Menten kinetics and the Nernst equation live!",
  "quickGuideGlosThetaTitle": "θ — Volumetric Water Content (m³/m³)",
  "quickGuideGlosThetaBody": "Soil water volume relative to total soil volume. 0.05 = permanent wilting point, 0.25 - 0.35 = optimal field capacity, > 0.45 = near-saturation or hypoxic flood state.",
  "quickGuideGlosKsatTitle": "kSat — Saturated Hydraulic Conductivity (m/s)",
  "quickGuideGlosKsatBody": "Capacity of soil to transmit water under saturated conditions. High in coarse sand (10⁻⁴ m/s = fast drainage), very low in compacted clay (10⁻⁷ - 10⁻⁹ m/s = surface water ponding).",
  "quickGuideGlosEhTitle": "Eh — Redox Potential (mV)",
  "quickGuideGlosEhBody": "Quantifies oxidation-reduction status. Aerobic soil (+400...+600 mV) sustains healthy root respiration and nitrification. Anaerobic soil (< +200 mV) triggers denitrification, iron reduction, and methanogenesis (-150 mV).",
  "quickGuideGlosSomTitle": "MAOM vs. POM — Organic Carbon Fractions",
  "quickGuideGlosSomBody": "MAOM (Mineral-Associated Organic Matter) is mineral-stabilized microbial carbon persisting for decades. POM (Particulate Organic Matter) is coarse, rapidly cycling plant particulate matter.",
  "quickGuideGlosCnTitle": "C/N Ratio — Carbon to Nitrogen Stoichiometry",
  "quickGuideGlosCnBody": "Determines balance between N mineralization and immobilization. When C/N > 25 (e.g. straw), microbes lock up all mineral N. When C/N < 20 (e.g. legumes or manure), plant-available nitrogen is released.",
  "quickGuideGlosParTitle": "PAR — Photosynthetically Active Radiation (µmol m⁻² s⁻¹)",
  "quickGuideGlosParBody": "Spectral waveband 400–700 nm absorbed by chlorophyll for carbon fixation (FvCB model). Direct midsummer sun reaches ~1500–2000 µmol m⁻² s⁻¹.",
  "quickGuideGlosStomataTitle": "Stomatal Conductance & Turgor",
  "quickGuideGlosStomataBody": "Stomatal pore conductance controls transpiration and CO₂ uptake. Under water deficit, cell turgor pressure drops and stomata close to prevent desiccation.",
  "quickGuideGlosRichardsTitle": "Richards Equation & van Genuchten",
  "quickGuideGlosRichardsBody": "Nonlinear partial differential equation describing water movement in unsaturated porous media, linking gravitational flow and matric suction (van Genuchten curve).",
